<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Younghusband</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Francis Younghusband</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Younghusband was a British army officer and explorer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 1903 and 1904 he led the British expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, which killed hundreds of poorly armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> at Chumik Shenko, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and imposed a treaty opening Tibet to British trade. He later became president of the Royal Geographical Society and promoted the early Everest expeditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband.jpg" alt="Francis Younghusband" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lewis, Clifton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_Younghusband_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francis Edward Younghusband was born on 31 May 1863 at Murree, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, into a military family. His uncle Robert Shaw was a noted explorer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. He was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as an infant and educated by two aunts at Freshford, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, before entering Clifton College in 1876 and the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, in 1881. He was commissioned in the 1st King's Dragoon Guards in May 1882.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1886 and 1887, on leave, Younghusband travelled across Asia from Peking, exploring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and then crossing the Taklamakan Desert to Chinese Turkestan. He pioneered a route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through the Mustagh Pass. For this he was elected the youngest member of the Royal Geographical Society and received its Patron's Medal in 1890. In 1889 he was sent with Gurkha soldiers to investigate an uncharted region north of Ladakh, where he dined with his Russian counterpart Captain Bronislav Grombchevsky, and later dealt with Safdar Ali, the ruler of Hunza. He joined the Indian Political Service and spent the winter of 1890 at Kashgar with George Macartney, taking part in the rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>.</p><p>In 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> appointed him to head the Tibet Frontier Commission, with the stated aim of settling disputes over the Sikkim and Tibet border. The expedition exceeded its instructions and became in effect an invasion. Near Guru, troops with rifles and machine guns routed 600 to 700 Tibetan troops armed with hoes, swords and flintlocks. The expedition lost 202 men in action and 411 to other causes. The Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> that Younghusband signed with Tibetan officials was repudiated by the British government, which feared for its trade and relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. He was made Knight Commander of the Order of the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in December 1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Younghusband served as British Resident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 1906 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1909. He was president of the Royal Geographical Society from 1919 and chaired the Mount Everest Committee from 1921, which coordinated the early British expeditions. After a mystical experience during his retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> he turned to religious writing, advanced ideas of a living universe, and founded the World Congress of Faiths in 1936. He suffered a stroke in July 1942 after addressing one of its meetings and died on 31 July 1942 at Lytchett Minster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis Younghusband” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British expedition under <b>Younghusband</b> reached Lhasa in 1904, and Qing troops occupied it in 1910, driving the Thirteenth Dalai Lama into exile in India.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Murree, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in Lytchett Minster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.9,73.39&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Murree on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6421 935 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis Younghusband"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6871 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6871 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.9,73.39&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
